`specs/brief.md` describes the volume and its coordinate system, the palette, the
form of the fortress, the required parts and their pivots, and the required joints:
**twelve leg joints** — a `hip_*` and a `knee_*` per leg (`lf, lm, lr, rf, rm, rr`)
so each of the six legs swings and bends independently and lifts its foot clear of
the ground; the four gun joints — `main_turret_yaw` and `main_gun_pitch` for the
main turret and cannon, and `left_turret_yaw` / `right_turret_yaw` for the two
independently traversing side turrets; and the one auto `radar_spin` joint that
sweeps the decorative radar on its own. Run `voxel-anim --help` for the exact
operation and rig contract.

## What to deliver

Build and rig the fortress up operation by operation until each `parts/<part>.png`
matches the brief, the assembled `scene/` previews show the parts fitting together
correctly, and the rig is complete: a tiered, prowed armored citadel raised on six
independent two-jointed legs, carrying a big main turret and cannon, a side-mounted
turret out on each sponson, and a sweeping radar vane. The required interface is
**sixteen caller joints** — the twelve leg joints (`hip_*` swing each leg,
`knee_*` bends each shin to lift the foot), plus `main_turret_yaw` (fine-aim within
a narrow forward cone), `main_gun_pitch` (elevate the cannon), and `left_turret_yaw`
/ `right_turret_yaw` (traverse each side turret across its own flank) — and **one
auto joint**, `radar_spin`, that sweeps the radar on its own. Produce **every**
required part and joint; you may add your own extra parts, joints, or auto-play
clips on top, but do not drop or contradict the required interface. When you are
satisfied, stop: the recorded per-part logs and `rig.json` are your finished
submission.
